  <section class="synthesis" aria-labelledby="synthesis-title">
    <div class="label">Selected reading</div>
    <h2 id="synthesis-title">What is a public form permitted to do — and what must it not claim?</h2>
    <p class="question">The four states can be read together through a shared constraint: <strong>how can a changing system make bounded parts of itself encounterable without allowing each new public form to stand in for the whole?</strong></p>
    <p class="permissions-scope">Across the four repository states examined here:</p>
    <div class="permissions">
      <div class="permission"><strong>Anchor</strong><span>may become findable, but not stand for the whole.</span></div>
      <div class="permission"><strong>Freeze</strong><span>may remain verifiable, but not be rewritten.</span></div>
      <div class="permission"><strong>Accumulate</strong><span>may be joined by a later state, but not automatically superseded.</span></div>
      <div class="permission"><strong>Recompose</strong><span>may reorganize the arrangement of the presented material, but not thereby acquire formal semantic authority.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="formula"><strong>Reading shorthand:</strong><span>public form × permission × authority ceiling</span></p>
    <p class="boundary-note">This synthesis is a reading across four specific repository states. Chronological order does not establish causation, and one demonstrated instance does not automatically become a system-wide principle.</p>
  </section>

